The Red junglefowl is the main ancestor of the chicken we keep today [3]. In the wild, it lives in damp tropical areas of South and South-East Asia. Village chickens are different. They have adapted very well to many kinds of surroundings, including dry and fairly dry areas. They also come in a wide range of body shapes and appearances, and some of these features may be linked to coping with hot, dry places. Examples include the "naked neck", where the skin is bare instead of covered in feathers, a small body, and frizzled feathers that curl outwards [4–6]. Recent studies of the whole set of genes in these chickens have found stretches of DNA that seem to have been favoured by natural selection, which may be linked to how the birds have adapted to their surroundings [7].

Scientists have used the DNA of mothers' family lines a great deal to work out the history of domestic chickens [8]. Reading this DNA lets us identify the wild ancestor or ancestors of a breed or population, and the mother's family lines that have contributed to it [9, 10]. It can also tell us a lot about how people carried chickens away from the places where they were first tamed [10]. Finally, knowing how much variety this DNA holds can help people plan good ways to manage chickens and to protect their genetic variety in a lasting way.

In this study, we set out to uncover the history and genetic variety of local chickens from the Middle East, northern Africa and the Horn of Africa. We included chickens from five areas:

(i) Pakistan, which may be an ancient place where domestic chickens first came from, in the northern part of the Indian subcontinent [11];

(ii) Iraq, home to the ancient Mesopotamian civilisations, which were in contact with those of the Indus Valley;

(iii) Saudi Arabia, on the route to the African continent;

(iv) the Horn of Africa, represented here by Ethiopia, where the oldest chicken bones found in Africa date from before the Aksumite kingdom [12];

(v) Algeria and Libya, two countries on the Mediterranean Sea, where ancient Phoenician, Greek and Roman traders met by land and by sea, linking North Africa, the Near East and Europe [13].

In total, we analysed 706 D-loop sequences. We compared them with Asian DNA versions (haplotypes) from earlier work by Liu and colleagues [14].
